  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Monsoon moisture drives thunderstorms through Thursday, bring\n   a localized flash flood threat.\n\n-  Hot, dry, and breezy conditions this weekend will be a concern\n   for critical fire weather conditions.\n\n-  Smoke from the Pacific Northwest wildfires will continue to\n   impact air quality, potentially worsening over the weekend. \n\nThe Southwest Monsoon continues to build a ridge of high pressure over the\nwestern U.S. while drawing in abundant moisture. Today and Thursday, that\nmoisture will work its way into southern Idaho, all of Lemhi County in\nIdaho, and southwest Montana. The result will be widespread showers and\nthunderstorms in these regions. The abundant moisture and slow-moving\nnature of these storms make heavy rain and localized flash flooding the\nprimary risks.\n\nLate Thursday and into the weekend, a broad area of low pressure over the\nnortheast Pacific Ocean pushes the high-pressure ridge further east,\nbringing in much drier air. This low-pressure system will also bring\nstronger winds aloft over the Northern Rockies, which will in turn produce\ngusty westerly winds over western Montana and north-central Idaho on Friday\nand Saturday. Currently, Saturday looks like the windier day, with\nwidespread gusts between 15 to 30 mph. There is a 20 to 30 percent\nprobability gusts will reach 40 mph in the terrain and valleys of western\nMontana Saturday afternoon and evening. These winds, along with expected\nrelative humidity values in the upper single digits and low teens, will\npresent a major concern for critical fire weather conditions.\n\nAnother potential problem with the westerly winds will be \nwidespread smoke from several wildfires currently burning in \nWashington and Oregon.",
